{"kind":"assert","text":"The first \"Scale witnesses on main\" run failed at main 4f06bbbc: run 42570561287, ubuntu-24.04, 06:01 to 06:22 UTC. Five of the six cases passed. One case reached its own deadline:\n\n- floor-archive.test.ts:67, \"Native alarm writer advances a30000-entry journal in five bounded floors then waits for a1000-position target rise\", failed with \"Test timed out in 600000ms\". The second annotation, \"Native archive fixture ended\" at archive-native-authority.ts:96, is the fixture teardown after that timeout. No assertion failed.\n\nThe runner ran every case 2.5 to 3.5 times slower than the repaired local gate at 4393c9fd. The durations below are observed in each run:\n\n| Case | Local gate | GitHub runner |\n|---|---|---|\n| 10,001 one-entry passes | 227 s | 724 s |\n| 30,000-entry journal | 211 s | over 600 s (deadline) |\n| 2,000-entry ceiling | 65 s | 224 s |\n| three windows of 4,800 entries | 75 s | 233 s |\n| 1,000 partition births | 189 s | 475 s |\n\nI read this as a runner-speed result, not a regression: BR-5 left the four owners byte-equal, and the same case passed at 211 s in the gate. The main run therefore cannot yet catch changes through the shared inputs that ruling ea2e17ab leaves off the trigger list, because it fails on every push.\n\nTwo options need a ruling: (1) run the workflow on a larger runner, or (2) raise this case's deadline for the main run only. Option 2 changes a witness's limit, so it needs your ruling under BR-5's acceptance. I recommend option 1, because it keeps every deadline. No deployment depends on this run.","body":{"to":"planner"}}